  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe are approaching this problem by using coordinate descent to optimize a sequence representation under convex constraints. Since the previous program failed to meet the target lower bound, we switch to an algorithm that explicitly handles the simplex constraint (non-negative elements summing to sqrt(2*n)) and directly minimizes the L2/L1 norm of coefficients, which is a known approach in compressed sensing.\n\nThe key observation is that the evaluation function is homogeneous of degree 0 in the sequence, meaning we can focus on sequences normalized by summing to sqrt(2*n). We implement a coordinate descent algorithm where each coordinate is updated one-by-one with projection to the simplex. This method is simple and ensures strict non-negativity, which is critical because the previous program's gradient descent relied on clipping.\n\nWe calculate the gradient numerically for each coordinate by introducing a small perturbation and use backtracking line search to determine the optimal step size. Unlike previous gradient-based methods that only clipped below 1000, we ensure the entire sequence stays within [0, 1000] bounds. The core innovation is the explicit enforcement of the simplex constraint and the numerical gradient computation with projection.\n\nWe consider two initialization strategies: when a parent sequence is given and when it's not. For given parent sequences, we clip and normalize them. For cases without a parent, we initialize with uniform sequences across fixed-length candidates. Finally, we validate the best candidate by re-evaluating it using the exact provided evaluator function.\n\nMathematical specification\n- Input representaton: A list of n real numbers.\n- Variables: We have n-dimensional vector x (representing the sequence) with:\n  - Non-negative components: x_i \u2265 0\n  - Sum x = S = sqrt(2*n)\n- Exact objective: minimize score = (2*n * max(convolve(x,x))) / (x_sum\u00b2) with the provided evaluation function.\n- Constraints: We maintain sum(x) = S and all x_i \u2265 0 during gradient descent steps using projection.\n- Optimization: Use coordinate descent with numerical gradients and projection to simplex after each coordinate update.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization with parent sequence (if exists):\n   - Read parent sequence (height_sequence_1) if available\n   - Clip parent elements to [0,1000] and compute initial sum\n   - For each length n in {2048}, check if sum > 0.01; if not, discard\n   - Save the initial clipped and normalized sequence\n\n2. When no parent:\n   - Set n to 256 (and consider variations: 2048, 512, 4096)\n   - Generate a uniform sequence [sqrt(2*n)/n for each element]\n   - Check validity with evaluate_sequence (must be finite)\n   - If implies 2.0, consider optimization: otherwise set fallback\n\n3. Optimization using coordinate descent:\n   - Normalize sequence to sum S=sqrt(2*n)\n   - For each coordinate i (from 0 to n-1):\n     - Store x_{-i} (neighbors)\n     - Compute candidate score (if max_conv is infinite, skip)\n     - Compute numerical gradient using finite differences\n     - Perform backtracking line search\n     - Update and project to simplex\n\n4. If no improvement:\n   - Switch to stochastic optimization if no parent\n   - Otherwise return best found sequence (even if 2.0)\n\n5. Validation:\n   - Use evaluate_sequence strictly on the best sequence\n   - Ensure all elements are within [0,1000] for finite scores\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Default length: 256 (fallback if no improvement)\n- Clip and normalize parent sequence before optimization\n- Length variations: 768, 1024 if time allows\n- Bounded sequence [0,1000] enforced via clipping\n- Number of iterations: 400 if time allows (max per coordinate)\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- n=256 sequence: 3 elements, each 4-8 bytes, 0.3 MB\n- Time for coordinate descent: n_inner iterations * (conv n=256 + evaluator)\n- Convolution and evaluate_sequence's convolve take >0.1s each\n- Each coordinate descent step: <0.8 seconds computation\n- Total run time: 400*8 = 3200 seconds limit, so we must stop when 2400s is up\n- We use validator below and validator above approach only for simplex projection\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Use evaluate_sequence to return final score for candidate\n- Best candidate is stored in best_sequence variable\n- Return [0.0,outside_softconv]\n- Use fallback uniform if no blackfound\n- Ensure all elements are finite and non-negative\n\nImplementation note:\n- Use simple loops and numpy for convolutions\n- Evaluate_sequence is called only when needed\n- Backtracking line search to ensure numerical stability\n- Project coordinates to simplex after every update",
